    $astroMet_master$> Every other object in this course has been studied at a distance. We infer the interiors of the gas giants from the wobble of a radio signal; we read the surfaces of moons from reflected light; we date craters by counting them. This chapter is different, and the difference is the whole point. Here, for the first time, we are holding the thing. A meteorite is a piece of another world that has arrived on a laboratory bench, where it can be sliced, ionised, mass-analysed, and dated to a fraction of a percent — and what those pieces tell us is not a refinement of the story built from telescopes but its foundation, because the oldest of them fix the moment the story begins. The trouble is that a rock that falls out of the sky arrives anonymous: shock-heated by its parent's last collision, seared by atmospheric entry, rusted by terrestrial weather, and colonised by terrestrial life within hours of landing, with no label saying where it came from or what it sat next to. A meteorite is a sample with no address. The second half of this chapter is about the decade in which we learned to fix that — to fly to a named body, take a documented handful of it, and bring it home untouched by air or biology — and about what the first such samples turned out to contain: the fragile, soluble, contamination-prone chemistry that a meteorite fall destroys, and that turns out to be exactly the chemistry astrobiology needs.

## What survives the fall

Start with the raw fact that this is even possible. Of the mass that the small bodies discussed earlier deliver to Earth, a tiny fraction arrives in lumps large enough to reach the ground, survive, and be recovered. Those lumps are **meteorites**, and they are, materially, the most information-dense objects in planetary science. A gram of the right meteorite has been dated more precisely than any rock on the Moon, carries grains older than the Sun, and preserves chemistry from the first hundred thousand years of the solar system's existence. No spacecraft image competes with that.

But the fall is violent, and it edits the record. An object entering the atmosphere at 15 to 70 kilometres per second is heated on its surface to incandescence; the outer millimetres ablate away and form a black glassy **fusion crust**, and the interior, though it stays cold, is subjected to a thermal and mechanical shock. What lands is then exposed to oxygen, rain, and biology. An ordinary chondrite left in a wet climate is visibly rusting within years and biologically overgrown within days. The pristine ones — the ones this chapter leans on — are the **falls** picked up promptly, and the **finds** preserved by accident in deserts and Antarctic ice, where cold and dryness slow the terrestrial editing to a crawl. Antarctica alone has yielded tens of thousands of specimens, concentrated by the flow of ice into a handful of blue-ice fields where the wind strips the snow and leaves the stones.

Keep in mind, throughout, the distinction that organises everything: some of these rocks were **never melted**, and some were **melted thoroughly.** That single split — did the parent body get hot enough to differentiate, to separate metal from rock and sink an iron core, or did it not? — is the backbone of meteorite classification and the first thing a meteorite tells you about where it came from.

## The great divide: chondrites and everything else

### Chondrites — the rock that never melted

A **chondrite** is a meteorite that has never been melted as a whole. It is a sediment: a physical accumulation of the small solid particles that were floating in the solar nebula — silicate dust, metal grains, sulphides, and the millimetre-scale spheres called chondrules from which the class takes its name — pressed together into rock without ever being heated to the point where those components would flow into one another and mix. That is an extraordinary thing to have. It means a chondrite is a **frozen sample of the disk itself**, its ingredients still sitting side by side in the proportions the disk delivered them.

The proof that they never melted is compositional, and it is one of the most quietly astonishing measurements in the field. Take the most primitive chondrites — the **CI carbonaceous chondrites**, of which only a handful of stones exist — and measure the relative abundances of the elements. Then measure the same abundances in the **Sun's photosphere**, by spectroscopy. For everything except the handful of elements that are gases or form gases — hydrogen, helium, carbon, nitrogen, oxygen, the noble gases — the two lists **agree, element by element, across more than a factor of a billion in abundance.** The rock in your hand has the composition of the Sun with the volatiles boiled off. This is why CI chondrites, not any terrestrial or lunar rock, define the **cosmochemical abundance standard** — the reference against which every other object's composition is measured for enrichment or depletion. A body that had ever melted and differentiated would have fractionated its elements and lost this match instantly. The match survives only because the rock is an unmelted average of the disk.

{{image: Chondrite | A cut and polished slice of an ordinary chondrite. The rounded millimetre-scale chondrules, embedded in a fine-grained matrix with flecks of metallic iron-nickel, are the once-molten droplets that make up most of the mass; the rock as a whole was never melted, only compacted.}}

Chondrites divide into three great clans, and the divisions are not arbitrary — they map onto **where in the disk, and under what conditions, the material assembled.**

- **Carbonaceous chondrites** are the volatile-rich, water-bearing, organic-bearing primitives, formed farther out and colder. The **CI** and **CM** subgroups are the most altered by water and the most interesting for what follows: they carry clays, carbonates, magnetite, and up to a few percent organic carbon, including amino acids. They are the least-cooked material we have.
- **Ordinary chondrites** are exactly that — ordinary, in the sense of abundant. They make up the overwhelming majority of all meteorite falls: dry, chondrule-rich rock from the inner belt, subdivided by iron content into the H, L, and LL groups.
- **Enstatite chondrites** are the oddballs formed under extremely reducing conditions — so little oxygen available that even elements that "should" be oxides sit as metal or sulphide. Their oxygen isotopes match Earth's almost exactly, which has made them a leading candidate for the kind of material Earth was actually built from, and a key clue in the isotopic-provenance work discussed below.

The reason chondrites can preserve all this is that their parent bodies stayed small and stayed cool. Heat one enough and the record is erased. Which brings us to the other half of the divide.

### Achondrites, irons, and stony-irons — the melted worlds

Everything that is not a chondrite comes from a parent body that got hot enough to melt, and once a rocky body of that size melts, physics takes over and sorts it. Dense metallic iron and nickel sink; silicate rock floats; a crust forms on top. The body **differentiates**, exactly as Earth did, into core, mantle, and crust. Break such a body apart in a later collision, and the fragments you get depend on which layer they came from.

- **Achondrites** are fragments of the **silicate crust and mantle** of a differentiated body — igneous rock, melted and recrystallised, with the chondrules and metal long since destroyed by the melting. They are basalts and related rocks from other worlds. A meteorite is an achondrite precisely because its parent finished the process a chondrite never started.
- **Iron meteorites** are fragments of the **metallic core** — nearly pure iron-nickel alloy, the sunk metal of a body whose rock has been stripped away. To hold an iron meteorite is to hold a piece of a planetesimal's core, exposed by the catastrophic disruption of everything that once lay above it.
- **Stony-irons** are the transition zones. **Pallasites** are a mixture of iron-nickel metal and gem-quality olivine crystals — most naturally read as samples of the **core-mantle boundary**, where sinking metal met floating silicate. **Mesosiderites** are a more chaotic breccia of metal and crustal silicate, probably the wreckage of a collision that smashed core material into crust.

Here is the payoff of the whole classification, stated as a single inference. When you sort the world's meteorite collections, you find fragments of the cores, mantles, crusts, and unmelted primitive surfaces of a great many distinct parent bodies. The **iron meteorites alone sample roughly a hundred chemically distinct parent cores.** That means the early solar system built, and then destroyed, at least a hundred bodies large enough and hot enough to differentiate — and it did so early, because the heat source that melted them was short-lived. The melting was driven principally by **aluminium-26**, a radioactive isotope with a half-life of only about 717,000 years, live in the disk at the start and gone within a few million. A planetesimal that accreted early, while $^{26}\text{Al}$ was still hot, melted and differentiated; one that accreted a few million years later, after the isotope had decayed, stayed a cold chondritic pile forever. Differentiation was a race against a radioactive clock, and the meteorite classes are the finishing positions.

## The zero point of the calendar

Now to three results that belong to no other chapter in this course, because they can only be obtained by holding the rock. The first sets the origin of time.

### CAIs, and the first solids

Cut open a carbonaceous chondrite like **Allende** — a large fall recovered in Mexico in 1969, and one of the most studied rocks in the world — and among the chondrules you find pale, irregular inclusions a few millimetres across, rich in calcium, aluminium, and titanium. These are **calcium-aluminium-rich inclusions**, or CAIs, and thermodynamics identifies them immediately: they are made of exactly the minerals that condense **first** from a cooling gas of solar composition — corundum, melilite, spinel, perovskite, the most refractory oxides there are, the ones that come out of the vapour above about 1,400 kelvin before anything else. They are the first solids. They are what the disk made before it made anything you could stand on.

{{image: Calcium–aluminium-rich inclusion | A calcium-aluminium-rich inclusion in the Allende meteorite. Composed of the most refractory minerals — the first to condense from a cooling gas of solar composition — CAIs are the oldest solids known, dated to 4.567 billion years, and their formation defines time zero for the solar system.}}

And they are the oldest solids known. Dated, they come out at **4.567 billion years** — and that number is not an approximation. It is measured to a precision of better than a million years, and it is the **zero of the solar system's calendar.** Every age you have met in this course — the crystallisation ages of lunar basalts, the crater-count chronologies, the differentiation ages of the iron parent bodies, the age of Earth itself — is quoted relative to this instant. When we say the Moon formed "60 million years after the start of the solar system," the start is the condensation of the CAIs. They are the fiducial. Fixing them was one of the load-bearing measurements of twentieth-century science.

### How you date a 4.567-billion-year-old rock

The tool is **radiometric dating**, and the specific method that reaches this precision is **lead-lead (Pb-Pb) dating.** The logic is worth understanding, because it is the logic behind nearly every absolute age in the solar system.

Uranium decays to lead, and it does so along two independent chains at once. **Uranium-238** decays to **lead-206** with a half-life of 4.47 billion years; **uranium-235** decays to **lead-207** with a half-life of only 704 million years. Two clocks, two rates, running in the same mineral grain from the moment it crystallised and locked in its uranium. The amount of each daughter lead isotope that has accumulated is set by the decay law,

$$\frac{^{206}\text{Pb}^*}{^{238}\text{U}} = e^{\lambda_{238} t} - 1, \qquad \frac{^{207}\text{Pb}^*}{^{235}\text{U}} = e^{\lambda_{235} t} - 1,$$

where the asterisk denotes lead produced by decay (radiogenic) and $\lambda = \ln 2 / t_{1/2}$ is the decay constant of each chain. The trick of the Pb-Pb method is to **divide one equation by the other.** The present-day ratio of the two uranium isotopes is a known constant of nature, $^{238}\text{U}/^{235}\text{U} \approx 137.8$ everywhere in the solar system, so

$$\frac{^{207}\text{Pb}^*}{^{206}\text{Pb}^*} = \frac{1}{137.8}\cdot\frac{e^{\lambda_{235} t} - 1}{e^{\lambda_{238} t} - 1}.$$

The right-hand side depends only on the age $t$. Measure the ratio of the two radiogenic lead isotopes — a ratio, not an absolute amount, which is what makes it so precise, because mass spectrometers measure isotope ratios far better than they measure quantities — and you can solve for $t$. You never need to know how much uranium or lead the sample started with. The two clocks calibrate each other. Run this on the CAIs and the answer, sharpened over decades, is $t = 4{,}567.3 \pm 0.2$ million years. The uncertainty is a hundredth of a percent on the age of the solar system.

## The droplets we cannot explain

The second result is a problem, not an answer, and it sits inside the most abundant meteorite class of all.

Look again at an ordinary chondrite. Most of its mass — often more than half — is made of **chondrules**: millimetre-scale spheres of silicate, frozen droplets, each one a bead of rock that was once **completely molten** and then cooled and solidified as a free-floating sphere in space. Their shapes and their internal crystal textures are unambiguous on this point. A chondrule is a splash of melt that surface tension pulled into a ball before it froze. There are octillions of them; they are a dominant solid component of the inner solar nebula.

{{image: Chondrule | A backscattered-electron image of chondrules in a chondrite. Each rounded object was a fully molten silicate droplet that cooled and crystallised in free space; their internal textures require heating to about 1,800 kelvin followed by cooling over hours, an event repeated for a large fraction of all inner-disk solids by a process still unidentified.}}

To make one, you must take a clump of nebular dust, **flash-heat it to roughly 1,800 kelvin** — hot enough to melt silicate completely — hold it there for minutes at most, and then let it **cool over the course of hours**, at rates of order tens to thousands of kelvin per hour, before it solidifies. Then you must do this **again and again**, for a large fraction of all the solid material in the inner disk, over a span of a few million years. The textures record the cooling rate directly: too slow and you would grow coarse crystals; too fast and you would quench to glass; the observed intermediate textures pin the rate to that hours-long window. And there is a second constraint hidden in that number. A millimetre droplet radiating freely into cold space cools not in hours but in **seconds** — you will estimate this in the problems — so chondrules cannot have cooled alone in the vacuum. They must have formed in regions dense enough with other hot solids and gas that the surrounding radiation field held the cooling down to the leisurely rate the textures demand. Whatever made chondrules made them in crowds.

And here is the honest admission the house voice requires: **we do not know what did this.** After more than a century of study of the most abundant extraterrestrial material we possess, the heating mechanism that made most of the mass of ordinary chondrites is unidentified. The candidates are all plausible and none is established:

- **Shock waves** in the nebular gas — driven by gravitational instabilities in the disk, or by the bow shocks of large planetesimals on eccentric orbits — that briefly heat and then release the dust they sweep through.
- **Lightning** in the disk, discharging static charge built up in the dusty gas.
- **The X-wind** — a model in which solids are lifted near the young Sun, flash-heated, and flung back out to the belt.
- **Planetesimal collisions** — molten splash from impacts between already-warm bodies.

Each explains some chondrule properties and struggles with others. It is a first-order unsolved problem — not a detail, but the origin of the single most abundant thing in the meteorite record — and it sits, unresolved, at the centre of the rock. That a science precise enough to date a grain to a hundredth of a percent cannot say what melted the grain next to it is a useful reminder of how the field's confidence is distributed.

## Grains older than the Sun

The third result should not be possible, and its existence is one of the great vindications of careful laboratory work.

Dissolve a primitive chondrite in acid — literally, dissolve away almost the entire rock, and examine the tiny residue that refuses to dissolve — and you find microscopic grains of **silicon carbide, graphite, and nanodiamond**, a few tens of nanometres to a few microns across. Measure their isotopic composition, atom by atom, in a sensitive mass spectrometer, and the numbers are **impossible.** The ratios of carbon isotopes, of silicon isotopes, of nitrogen, of the noble gases trapped inside these grains, are not slightly unusual — they are off by factors of hundreds or thousands from anything the solar system contains. No process operating in this system, then or since, can produce them.

{{image: Presolar grains | A microscopic silicon carbide grain extracted from a primitive meteorite. Its isotopic ratios lie hundreds to thousands of times outside the solar system's range, identifying it as a presolar grain — stardust that condensed in the outflow of a dying star before the Sun existed, and survived intact inside the rock ever since.}}

The only explanation is that these grains did not form here. They are **presolar grains** — stardust, condensed in the cooling outflows of stars that died before the Sun was born, and swept into the collapsing cloud from which the solar system formed. Each isotopic signature is a fingerprint of its stellar factory: grains with heavy-element patterns built by slow neutron capture point to the winds of **AGB stars**, the bloated red giants that dredge freshly-made carbon to their surfaces and blow it off; grains laced with the products of explosive nucleosynthesis point to **supernovae**. These are physical samples of other stars, delivered to a laboratory bench, older than the ground you stand on. They survived because their parent chondrite never got hot enough to destroy them — the same coolness that let CI chondrites keep the Sun's composition let a few grains keep the signature of stars that died before the Sun switched on. You cannot get this from a telescope. You can only get it by dissolving the rock and reading what is left.

## A sample with no address

Set those three results beside the classification, and the power of meteorites is undeniable. But now the limitation, because it is the hinge of the chapter.

Some meteorites we **can** trace to a parent body, and the successes are real and worth stating precisely, because they are the exceptions that reveal the rule.

**The HED meteorites and Vesta.** There is a clan of achondrites — **howardites, eucrites, and diogenites**, collectively the HEDs — that share a single chemistry and a single oxygen-isotope signature, and whose reflectance spectrum, measured in the laboratory, matches the spectrum of one specific large asteroid: **4 Vesta.** The match was proposed from ground-based spectroscopy and then confirmed decisively when the **Dawn** spacecraft orbited Vesta and found a giant impact basin at its south pole — Rheasilvia, a crater nearly as wide as the asteroid itself — excavated deep enough to expose the differentiated interior, with a family of smaller "vestoid" asteroids strung along a dynamical path from Vesta to a belt escape route. We have, in the HEDs, actual samples of the crust of a specific, named, differentiated protoplanet, and we have flown to that protoplanet and seen the wound they were blasted from.

**The SNC meteorites and Mars.** A second clan — **shergottites, nakhlites, and chassignites**, the SNCs — are young igneous rocks, some crystallised less than 200 million years ago, far too recent to have formed on any small asteroid that froze solid in the first tens of millions of years. They had to come from a body still geologically active recently: a planet. The proof came from the gas. Some shergottites contain pockets of glass, melted by the impact that launched them, and trapped inside that glass is a sample of the atmosphere the rock was sitting in when it was hit. Measure the noble gases and nitrogen in that trapped gas — the ratio of argon isotopes, the enrichment of the heavy nitrogen isotope, the abundances of xenon and krypton — and the pattern matches, point for point, the **Martian atmosphere as measured directly by the Viking landers** in 1976. The atmosphere sealed inside these rocks is the air of Mars. No other body in the solar system has that particular fingerprint. These are pieces of Mars, delivered free of charge.

**Lunar meteorites** close the set: rocks whose chemistry and mineralogy match the samples the Apollo missions brought back so exactly that their lunar origin is beyond dispute — and which, unlike the Apollo samples, come from random locations across the whole Moon rather than the handful of visited sites.

Now the limitation, stated flatly. These successes cover a small fraction of the collection. For the overwhelming majority of meteorites — including nearly every chondrite, the very rocks that carry the CAIs, the chondrules, and the presolar grains — **we do not know which body they came from.** And even where we can name the parent, the sample arrives degraded in exactly the ways that matter most. It has been:

- **shock-heated** by the collision that launched it, and again on entry, scrambling the fine thermal record;
- **stripped** of its most soluble and volatile components by atmospheric ablation;
- **weathered** by terrestrial water and oxygen, so that its own water-soluble minerals dissolve and its metal rusts;
- and **contaminated** by terrestrial biology within hours — amino acids, microbes, organic molecules of Earth crawling into the very cracks where its own organics are hiding.

A meteorite is a sample with no address, arriving battered, leached, rusted, and contaminated. For the coarse story — melted or not, iron or stone, roughly how old — that is enough. For the finest and most consequential question we can ask of these rocks — what fragile organic and aqueous chemistry did the early solar system actually make? — it is not remotely enough. The contamination alone is fatal: how could you ever prove that an amino acid in a meteorite that sat in a museum drawer for a century came from space and not from the hands that collected it? You could not. To answer that, you have to go and get a sample yourself, and never let the air or your own biology near it.

## Sample return: fixing the address

That is precisely what sample return does, and the last two decades have been its coming of age. The principle is simple to state and brutally hard to execute: fly to a **named** body, collect a **documented** amount of it, seal it against contamination, and bring it home **without ever passing it through an atmosphere.** Every one of the meteorite's four wounds is closed at once. Provenance is known to the boulder. Handling is curated in nitrogen from the moment of capture. There is no entry heating of the sample, because it rides home inside a sealed capsule that takes the entry heat on its own shield while the sample stays cold. And contamination is controlled from launch, with witness plates flown alongside to measure whatever terrestrial trace leaks in anyway.

The campaign, in order:

- **Genesis** (collected 2001–2004) flew not to a body but into the **solar wind**, exposing ultrapure collector wafers beyond the Moon to catch a sample of the Sun itself — ions streaming off the solar surface. Its capsule's parachute failed and it crashed into the Utah desert, shattering the collectors; heroic clean-room work nonetheless recovered enough to nail down the Sun's oxygen and nitrogen isotopes, a reference the whole field needed.
- **Stardust** (return 2006) flew through the coma of comet **81P/Wild 2**, catching grains of cometary dust in a block of aerogel as it passed at six kilometres per second, and parachuted them to Earth. The comet dust held a shock: crystalline silicates and even a CAI-like grain, high-temperature inner-disk material somehow transported out to the cold comet-forming zone — direct evidence of large-scale mixing in the early disk.
- **Hayabusa** (return 2010) touched the small stony asteroid **Itokawa** and, despite a cascade of failures, brought home the **first sample ever returned from an asteroid** — a few thousand microscopic grains that proved, by their mineralogy, that the abundant ordinary chondrites come from exactly this kind of common stony asteroid, closing a long-standing question about the meteorite-asteroid link.
- **Hayabusa2** (return 2020) brought back some five grams of the carbonaceous asteroid **Ryugu**, including subsurface material blasted out by a copper impactor and collected before sunlight could alter it.
- **OSIRIS-REx** (return 2023) brought back about 120 grams — by far the largest haul — of the carbonaceous asteroid **Bennu**, a rubble-pile body of the most primitive, water-rich type.

{{image: Hayabusa2 | The Hayabusa2 spacecraft, which returned about five grams of the carbonaceous asteroid Ryugu to Earth in December 2020. The sample was collected and sealed in vacuum and never passed through the atmosphere until it rode home inside a heat-shielded capsule, preserving soluble and organic compounds a meteorite fall would destroy.}}

{{image: OSIRIS-REx | The OSIRIS-REx sample-return capsule after landing in the Utah desert in September 2023, carrying about 120 grams of the asteroid Bennu. Curated in nitrogen from the moment of opening, the Bennu sample yielded amino acids, nucleobases, and evaporite salts — evidence of ancient brines that terrestrial weathering would have dissolved away.}}

## What the pristine samples held

The Ryugu and Bennu samples justified the entire enterprise, because they contained precisely the material a meteorite fall destroys.

Both are aqueously altered carbonaceous rock — the pristine, uncontaminated equivalents of CI/CM chondrites — and both turned out to be soaked, long ago, in liquid water. The samples are full of clays and carbonates and magnetite, the mineral signatures of **extensive aqueous alteration**: this material sat, on its parent body, in contact with liquid water for a long time, warmed by the last of the radioactive heat. And in the Bennu sample the record went further, to something a fall could never have preserved: **evaporite salts** — sodium carbonates, sulphates, phosphates, chlorides — the minerals that crystallise out when a **brine evaporates.** Those salts are wildly water-soluble. Any meteorite carrying them would have had them washed clean out by the first rain it met on Earth. Only a sample kept dry from the asteroid to the laboratory could still hold them, and they are direct chemical fossils of **ancient brines**, pools of salty water on a body a few hundred metres across, four and a half billion years ago.

Then the organics. Both samples carry a rich inventory of carbon chemistry, and crucially it was measured under conditions clean enough to trust: thousands of organic compounds, a suite of **amino acids** — including ones both common and rare in terrestrial biology — and, in Bennu, all five of the **nucleobases** that spell out the genetic alphabet of DNA and RNA, along with other components of biological molecules. This is not evidence of life; it is evidence of **chemistry** — that the un-living early solar system, in the wet interiors of small carbonaceous bodies, was already assembling the molecular feedstock that life on Earth would later use. And the reason it can be believed, where the identical claim from a meteorite always carried an asterisk, is the curation. When an amino acid appears in a rock that went from the surface of Bennu into a sealed nitrogen glovebox without ever touching air or hand, the contamination objection that has haunted meteorite organics for fifty years finally loses its force. Sample return did not find something meteorites lacked. It found something meteorites had all along and could never prove they had.

## Where the block ends

Step back to the scale of the whole argument the small-bodies chapters have been building. The gas giants and their moons showed that the outer solar system is not a dead freezer but a supplier of **habitats** — subsurface oceans, warmed by tides, walled in ice. The comets and asteroids showed that the same region supplies the **delivery**: water and carbon, carried inward across the snow line drawn early in the course, raining onto the young terrestrial planets. This chapter is where the two threads are handed on. The small bodies do not merely deliver water; they deliver, as Ryugu and Bennu now prove in curated detail, **water that already carried amino acids, nucleobases, and the salts of evaporated brine** — a prebiotic chemistry set, manufactured in the disk and shipped inward. That is the material astrobiology begins with, and the point at which this block passes the question along: given habitats out there and organic feedstock delivered here, what happens next is the astrobiology question, and it is no longer a question about rocks.

One quantitative correction before we close, because the intuition is usually wrong. When people picture the delivery of extraterrestrial material to Earth, they picture meteorites — the recoverable stones. But the recoverable stones are a rounding error in the **mass** budget. Earth accretes roughly forty thousand tonnes of extraterrestrial material a year, and the overwhelming majority of that mass arrives not as falls but as **dust and micrometeorites** — particles a fraction of a millimetre across, decelerated gently high in the atmosphere and settling unmelted to the surface, invisibly, everywhere, all the time. You will estimate the ratio in the problems; it is large. The dramatic fireball that drops a stone in a field is real, but it is not how the solar system mostly touches the Earth. The Earth is dusted, continuously, by the same disk the chondrites came from — and that quiet rain of fine, carbon-bearing, water-bearing dust may well have delivered more organic feedstock, integrated over the planet's history, than every fall combined.

## Further reading

The literature on meteorites is unusually welcoming, because the objects are tangible and the arguments are concrete. For a single authoritative and readable entry into how these rocks are read — classification, chondrules, CAIs, dating, and the presolar grains — {{book: Alex Bevan and John de Laeter | Meteorites: A Journey Through Space and Time | 2002}} remains an excellent starting point, written for the curious rather than the specialist. For the sample-return story specifically, the mission science teams have published their findings in the open literature, and the curation facilities' public reports on Ryugu and Bennu are readable and current. Beyond that, seek out the primary announcements of the Bennu organic and evaporite results, which are recent enough that no textbook yet contains them and which read, gratifyingly, like the working notes of people who cannot quite believe what they are holding.

- **Bevan, A. and de Laeter, J., *Meteorites: A Journey Through Space and Time* (2002).** A clear, non-technical account of what meteorites are, how they are classified, and what they reveal about the early solar system.

## Problems

*Constants and data you may need: decay constants $\lambda_{238} = 1.551\times10^{-10}\ \text{yr}^{-1}$ ($^{238}\text{U}\to{}^{206}\text{Pb}$, half-life 4.47 Gyr), $\lambda_{235} = 9.849\times10^{-10}\ \text{yr}^{-1}$ ($^{235}\text{U}\to{}^{207}\text{Pb}$, half-life 0.704 Gyr); present-day $^{238}\text{U}/^{235}\text{U} = 137.8$. Stefan–Boltzmann constant $\sigma = 5.67\times10^{-8}\ \text{W m}^{-2}\text{K}^{-4}$. Silicate density $\rho \approx 3{,}300\ \text{kg m}^{-3}$, specific heat $c \approx 1{,}000\ \text{J kg}^{-1}\text{K}^{-1}$, thermal diffusivity $\kappa \approx 5\times10^{-7}\ \text{m}^2\text{s}^{-1}$. 1 yr $= 3.156\times10^{7}$ s. Earth radius $6{,}371$ km. Problem 2 (the calendar's zero point) and problem 6 (why sample return beats a fall) are the conceptual core; problem 9 has no settled answer and is meant to be argued about.*

**1 — Reading the parent body.** For each meteorite below, state whether its parent body differentiated, and what part of that body (if any) the sample represents. **(a)** An ordinary chondrite. **(b)** A eucrite (an HED basalt). **(c)** An iron meteorite. **(d)** A pallasite. **(e)** In one sentence, explain what the very existence of iron meteorites from roughly a hundred distinct parent cores tells you about the first few million years of the solar system, and name the heat source responsible.

**2 — Why the CAIs are time zero.** **(a)** In thermodynamic terms, why are CAIs the *oldest* solids rather than merely *old* ones — what physical event does their formation mark? **(b)** Every absolute age in this course — lunar basalts, the iron parent bodies, Earth itself — is quoted relative to CAI formation. Why is it scientifically preferable to anchor the calendar to a measured physical event rather than to, say, "the present"? **(c)** CI chondrites match the Sun's photospheric composition element-for-element except for a specific set of elements. Which set, and why are exactly those excepted? **(d)** Explain how the CI–Sun match and the CAI age together support the claim that a chondrite is "a frozen sample of the disk."

**3 — Dating a CAI by lead-lead.** A CAI mineral separate is measured to have a radiogenic lead ratio $^{207}\text{Pb}^*/^{206}\text{Pb}^* = 0.625$. Using $$\frac{^{207}\text{Pb}^*}{^{206}\text{Pb}^*} = \frac{1}{137.8}\cdot\frac{e^{\lambda_{235} t} - 1}{e^{\lambda_{238} t} - 1},$$ **(a)** confirm by direct substitution that $t = 4.567$ Gyr reproduces the measured ratio. **(b)** Explain why this method needs no knowledge of the initial uranium or lead abundances, and why measuring a *ratio* of two lead isotopes is far more precise than measuring the quantity of lead. **(c)** The two uranium chains have very different half-lives (4.47 vs 0.704 Gyr). Which chain's clock has "run down" more over 4.567 Gyr, and why does having two clocks of different rates in the same grain make the method self-checking? **(d)** If a laboratory contaminant added a small amount of ordinary terrestrial lead (which is not purely radiogenic) to the sample, would the inferred age come out too old or too young? Reason it out.

**4 — The cooling of a chondrule.** Model a chondrule as an isolated silicate sphere of radius $r = 0.5$ mm at $T = 1{,}800$ K, radiating into cold space as a blackbody. **(a)** Show that its instantaneous cooling rate is $$\frac{dT}{dt} = -\frac{3\sigma T^4}{\rho c\, r},$$ and evaluate it in K s$^{-1}$ and K hr$^{-1}$. **(b)** Chondrule textures require cooling rates of order 10–1,000 K hr$^{-1}$. By what factor does the free-radiation estimate exceed even the fast end of that range? **(c)** What must have been true of a chondrule's surroundings for it to have cooled as slowly as the textures demand? State the physical condition in words. **(d)** What does this tell you about *where* in the disk chondrules formed — isolated, or in crowds? Connect your answer to the fact that chondrules make up most of the mass of ordinary chondrites.

**5 — Iron cooling rates and the size of a core's parent.** The Widmanstätten pattern in an iron meteorite records the rate at which the metal cooled through a few hundred kelvin around 700 K; the intergrowth widths imply cooling rates of order 10 K per million years. **(a)** Estimate the time $\tau$ the metal took to cool through a range $\Delta T \approx 200$ K at that rate, in seconds. **(b)** A buried metallic core cools by thermal diffusion; the relevant length scale is $\ell \approx \sqrt{\kappa \tau}$. Estimate $\ell$. **(c)** Hence give an order-of-magnitude for the radius of the parent body from which this iron came, and state whether it was planet-sized or planetesimal-sized. **(d)** Why does a slower measured cooling rate imply a *larger* parent body?

**6 — Why sample return beats a fall (the conceptual core).** A colleague argues that with tens of thousands of meteorites already curated for free, spending a billion dollars to bring back a hundred grams of asteroid is poor value. Rebut the argument on scientific grounds. **(a)** List the four distinct ways a meteorite is degraded relative to a returned sample, and for each, name one specific class of measurement it compromises. **(b)** The Bennu sample contained water-soluble evaporite salts and the Ryugu sample preserved unaltered subsurface organics. Explain why *neither* result could have been obtained from a meteorite of the same body, no matter how promptly it was recovered. **(c)** The single deepest advantage is not physical damage at all. What is it, and why does it specifically rescue the fifty-year-old controversy over amino acids in meteorites? **(d)** Give the one scientific question for which meteorites remain *superior* to sample return, and say why.

**7 — Pinning a rock to Mars.** A shergottite contains impact glass, and trapped inside that glass is a gas whose noble-gas and nitrogen isotope ratios match the Viking measurements of the Martian atmosphere. **(a)** Explain the physical event that sealed atmospheric gas into the glass, and why glass specifically. **(b)** The shergottites crystallised less than 200 Myr ago. Explain why that age *alone* already rules out an asteroidal origin, before any gas is measured. **(c)** Why is a *pattern* of several isotope ratios matching — argon, nitrogen, xenon, krypton together — a far stronger identification than any single ratio matching? Frame the argument in terms of how many bodies could coincidentally match one ratio versus five. **(d)** Contrast this identification with the HED–Vesta link: what independent line of evidence confirmed the HED–Vesta match that the SNC–Mars match (until a Mars sample returns) still lacks?

**8 — Stardust that shouldn't exist.** A grain of silicon carbide extracted from a chondrite has a carbon isotope ratio $^{12}\text{C}/^{13}\text{C}$ that differs from the solar system's value by a factor of several hundred. **(a)** Why does this ratio, by itself, establish that the grain did not form anywhere in the solar system? **(b)** What kind of environment did it form in, and how did it end up inside a meteorite? **(c)** These grains survive in some chondrites and not others. What property of the host rock determines whether presolar grains are preserved, and how does this connect to the same property that lets CI chondrites preserve the Sun's composition? **(d)** Explain why presolar grains are a capability unique to holding the rock — why no remote-sensing measurement, however good, could ever obtain them.

**9 — Open problem: what melted the chondrules, and what did the dust deliver?** Two unsettled questions bracket this chapter. **(a)** Chondrules required repeated flash-heating of most of the inner disk's solids to 1,800 K, by an unidentified mechanism. Lay out the leading candidates (shock waves, lightning, the X-wind, collisions) and, for each, name one observed chondrule property it explains well and one it struggles with. **(b)** Design the single measurement or dataset that would most cleanly discriminate between a *nebular* mechanism (shocks, lightning, X-wind) and an *impact* mechanism (collisions). Be specific about what each predicts. **(c)** Separately: the mass flux onto Earth is dominated by fine dust and micrometeorites, not falls (problem 8 of the previous chapter, and the estimate below). Argue whether the *organic* delivery to the early Earth was likely dominated by the same fine dust or by the rarer large impactors, and state what evidence would settle it. **(d)** Why are both of these questions ones that neither pure theory nor a single new spacecraft can close on its own?

## Worked answers

**1 — Reading the parent body.** **(a)** An ordinary chondrite: parent body **did not differentiate** — it never melted. The sample is a compacted, unmelted average of the disk's solids (chondrules, metal, matrix), representing the body as a whole, not any layer. **(b)** A eucrite: parent body **did differentiate**; the sample is a piece of the **silicate crust**, an igneous basalt from a melted, layered protoplanet (Vesta). **(c)** An iron meteorite: parent body **did differentiate**; the sample is a piece of the **metallic core**, the sunk iron-nickel of a body whose rock has been stripped away by collision. **(d)** A pallasite: **differentiated**; the metal-plus-olivine mixture most naturally samples the **core-mantle boundary**, where sinking metal met floating silicate. **(e)** The existence of iron fragments from ~100 distinct cores means the early solar system built and then destroyed at least a hundred bodies large enough and hot enough to melt and differentiate, all within the first few million years — the melting was driven by the short-lived radioisotope **aluminium-26** ($^{26}\text{Al}$, half-life ~0.72 Myr), so only bodies that accreted early, while it was still live, got hot enough to differentiate.

**2 — Why the CAIs are time zero.** **(a)** CAIs are made of the most refractory minerals — the first to condense from a cooling gas of solar composition, above ~1,400 K. Their formation marks the **first appearance of solid matter** in the disk: before them there was only gas and inherited dust; they are the first thing the solar nebula itself condensed. That is why they are the *oldest*, not merely old — nothing the system made could predate the system's first condensate. **(b)** A measured physical event is a **fixed, reproducible, universally shared fiducial**: every laboratory that dates a CAI gets the same instant, and "60 Myr after CAIs" means the same thing to everyone forever. "The present" drifts (it is a different instant every year) and is not marked in any rock. Anchoring to CAI formation ties the whole chronology to a single dated event that every sample can be measured against. **(c)** The excepted elements are the **volatiles** — hydrogen, helium, carbon, nitrogen, oxygen, and the noble gases — i.e. the elements that are gases or form gaseous compounds. They are excepted because they did not fully condense into the solid that became the chondrite; they stayed in the gas and were largely lost, so the rock is depleted in them relative to the Sun while matching the Sun in everything that condensed. **(d)** The CI–Sun match shows the rock is an **unfractionated average of the disk's condensable material** (any melting/differentiation would have destroyed the match); the CAI age shows that rock contains the disk's **first solids**, formed at the very start. Together: a chondrite is unmelted primordial disk material, assembled at time zero and never cooked since — a frozen sample of the disk.

**3 — Dating a CAI by lead-lead.** **(a)** With $t = 4.567\times10^{9}$ yr: $\lambda_{235}t = 9.849\times10^{-10}\times4.567\times10^{9} = 4.498$, so $e^{4.498} - 1 = 89.8 - 1 = 88.8$. $\lambda_{238}t = 1.551\times10^{-10}\times4.567\times10^{9} = 0.7084$, so $e^{0.7084} - 1 = 2.031 - 1 = 1.031$. Then $\dfrac{1}{137.8}\times\dfrac{88.8}{1.031} = \dfrac{86.1}{137.8} = \mathbf{0.625}$ — matching the measurement. **(b)** Dividing the two decay equations cancels the uranium terms; the ratio $^{207}\text{Pb}^*/^{206}\text{Pb}^*$ depends only on $t$ and the fixed constant $^{238}\text{U}/^{235}\text{U}$, never on how much U or Pb was present initially. And a mass spectrometer measures **isotope ratios** (two nearly identical masses in the same sample, same ionisation, same run) far more precisely than **absolute amounts** (which require knowing sample size, ionisation efficiency, and detector calibration) — so casting the age as a ratio-of-ratios buys the hundredth-of-a-percent precision. **(c)** The **$^{235}\text{U}\to{}^{207}\text{Pb}$** clock (half-life 0.704 Gyr) has run down far more: 4.567 Gyr is about 6.5 of its half-lives, so most of the original $^{235}\text{U}$ is gone, whereas 4.567 Gyr is only ~1.0 half-life of $^{238}\text{U}$. Two clocks of different rate ticking in the same grain must give the **same** $t$; if they disagree, the sample has been disturbed (lead loss, contamination), so the method flags its own failures rather than returning a false precise age. **(d)** Ordinary terrestrial lead is relatively rich in $^{206}\text{Pb}$ and $^{207}\text{Pb}$ but with a *lower* $^{207}/^{206}$ ratio than ancient radiogenic lead (because most $^{235}\text{U}$ decayed long ago, little $^{207}\text{Pb}$ has been made recently). Adding it lowers the measured $^{207}\text{Pb}^*/^{206}\text{Pb}^*$, and since the ratio *increases* with age, a lowered ratio yields an age that is **too young.** (This is why Pb-Pb work is done in extreme clean labs and why the raw data are corrected for a measured initial/common-lead component.)

**4 — The cooling of a chondrule.** **(a)** Energy balance: the sphere's heat content changes as it radiates. $mc\,\dfrac{dT}{dt} = -\varepsilon\sigma A T^4$ with $m = \rho\frac{4}{3}\pi r^3$, $A = 4\pi r^2$, $\varepsilon = 1$. So $\dfrac{dT}{dt} = -\dfrac{\sigma A T^4}{mc} = -\dfrac{\sigma\,4\pi r^2\,T^4}{\rho\frac{4}{3}\pi r^3\,c} = -\dfrac{3\sigma T^4}{\rho c\,r}.$ Evaluate: $T^4 = (1800)^4 = 1.05\times10^{13}$ K$^4$; numerator $3\sigma T^4 = 3\times5.67\times10^{-8}\times1.05\times10^{13} = 1.79\times10^{6}$; denominator $\rho c r = 3300\times1000\times5\times10^{-4} = 1.65\times10^{3}$. So $\dfrac{dT}{dt} = -\dfrac{1.79\times10^{6}}{1.65\times10^{3}} \approx \mathbf{-1{,}080\ K\,s^{-1}}$, i.e. about $\mathbf{-3.9\times10^{6}\ K\,hr^{-1}}$. The droplet would freeze in a second or so. **(b)** Against the fast end (1,000 K hr$^{-1}$), the free-radiation rate exceeds it by a factor of $\sim3.9\times10^{6}/10^{3} \approx \mathbf{4{,}000}$; against the slow end (10 K hr$^{-1}$), by $\sim4\times10^{5}$. **(c)** The chondrule cannot have been radiating into cold empty space. Its surroundings must have been **hot and optically thick** — a dense concentration of other hot solids and gas whose own thermal radiation bathed the droplet, so the *net* radiative loss was tiny and cooling proceeded at the slow rate the textures record. **(d)** Chondrules formed **in crowds**, in locally dense regions of the disk, not as isolated droplets — which is entirely consistent with the fact that they went on to make up most of the mass of ordinary chondrites. Whatever heating event fired them acted on dense swarms of solids, and the same swarms both slowed the cooling and supplied the material the chondrites are built from.

**5 — Iron cooling rates and the size of a core's parent.** **(a)** At 10 K per Myr, cooling through $\Delta T = 200$ K takes $20$ Myr $= 20\times10^{6}\times3.156\times10^{7}$ s $\approx \mathbf{6.3\times10^{14}\ s}$. **(b)** $\ell \approx \sqrt{\kappa\tau} = \sqrt{5\times10^{-7}\times6.3\times10^{14}} = \sqrt{3.2\times10^{8}} \approx \mathbf{1.8\times10^{4}\ m} \approx 18$ km. **(c)** The metal was buried at least this deep inside its parent, so the parent's radius is of order **tens of kilometres** — a **planetesimal**, not a planet. (Detailed metallurgical models give iron parent bodies of a few tens to ~100 km radius, consistent with this estimate.) **(d)** A slower cooling rate means the heat took longer to escape, which means the metal was more deeply insulated — a **larger** enclosing body. Cooling rate and burial depth are inversely linked: the deeper the metal sat, the longer the diffusion path, the slower it cooled. Reading the cooling rate off the crystal pattern therefore *weighs the parent body*.

**6 — Why sample return beats a fall.** **(a)** Four degradations, each compromising a class of measurement: **shock heating** (on launch and on entry) — scrambles the low-temperature thermal and organic record, compromising delicate thermochronometry and heat-sensitive organics; **ablation/atmospheric passage** — strips the outer material and volatiles, compromising surface and volatile-abundance measurements; **terrestrial weathering** — dissolves water-soluble minerals and rusts metal, compromising any measurement of soluble salts, primary metal, and original oxidation state; **biological/organic contamination** — floods the sample with terrestrial molecules within hours, compromising every measurement of indigenous organics and isotopes. **(b)** The **evaporite salts** are wildly water-soluble; the first terrestrial rain (or even humid air) after a fall would leach them out, so no meteorite of Bennu could retain them regardless of recovery speed — the damage is done by Earth's water before anyone finds the stone. The **subsurface organics** require material that never saw sunlight, oxygen, or heat; an atmospheric entry heats and oxidises the exterior and a fall exposes any interior on cracking, so "promptness" cannot substitute for never having entered an atmosphere at all. **(c)** The deepest advantage is **known, controlled provenance and curation** — the sample went from a named body into sealed nitrogen without touching air or hand, with witness plates recording any leak. This rescues the amino-acid controversy because the fatal objection to meteorite organics was always *contamination*: you could never prove an amino acid came from space rather than from the collector. A curated sample removes the objection by construction — the molecule was sealed before any hand could reach it. **(d)** Meteorites remain superior for **statistical breadth and sheer mass**: tens of thousands of specimens sample ~100+ parent bodies across every class, whereas sample return has visited a handful of bodies at gram scale. For questions about the *diversity* of the meteorite population, or requiring large amounts of material, the free collection wins — sample return trades breadth for pristine, addressed depth.

**7 — Pinning a rock to Mars.** **(a)** A large impact on Mars melted part of the rock; the melt quenched to **glass**, trapping a pocket of the ambient Martian atmosphere as it solidified (and the same impact launched the fragment to space). Glass specifically, because a rapidly quenched melt seals gas into a non-crystalline solid rather than letting it escape along grain boundaries — it is an airtight time capsule. **(b)** Crystallisation younger than 200 Myr requires a parent body that was still **volcanically/geologically active** that recently. Small asteroids froze solid within the first tens of millions of years (their $^{26}\text{Al}$ heat was long gone), so they cannot have produced young igneous rock; only a large, long-lived body — a planet — can. **(c)** Any one isotope ratio might be matched coincidentally by several bodies. But the probability that a *second* unrelated body independently matches a second ratio, a third, a fourth, and a fifth is the product of the individual (already small) coincidence probabilities — it collapses toward zero. A joint match across argon, nitrogen, xenon, and krypton is effectively a **fingerprint**: it identifies one specific atmosphere, because no other body shares that whole pattern. (This is the same logic as the helium/heat joint argument for the giant planets — an explanation constraining several independent observables at once is worth far more than one tuned to a single number.) **(d)** The HED–Vesta match was independently confirmed by **spacecraft**: Dawn orbited Vesta and found the giant south-polar impact basin (Rheasilvia) that exposed the differentiated interior and the vestoid fragments dynamically linking Vesta to a delivery route — a physical, imaged source for the samples. The SNC–Mars match rests on the atmospheric-gas fingerprint and orbital data but, until a Mars sample is returned and cross-matched, lacks that closed loop of *sample measured against material collected at the named source.*

**8 — Stardust that shouldn't exist.** **(a)** Every process that has operated in the solar system — nebular condensation, chondrule melting, planetary geology — started from one well-mixed reservoir with a single $^{12}\text{C}/^{13}\text{C}$ value and can shift it only slightly. A ratio off by a factor of several hundred cannot be produced by *any* in-system process; it can only be inherited from material that carried that ratio in from **outside**, formed under different nucleosynthesis. **(b)** It condensed in the cooling **outflow of a dying star** — the wind of an AGB red giant or the ejecta of a supernova — where freshly synthesised elements with exotic isotope ratios cooled enough to form solid grains. That stardust drifted through the interstellar medium into the molecular cloud that collapsed to form the Sun, and a few grains were swept into a parent body that never heated enough to destroy them. **(c)** Preservation depends on the host rock having **stayed cool** — never melted, minimally thermally metamorphosed, minimally aqueously altered. The same low-temperature history that lets CI chondrites keep the Sun's element abundances lets the most primitive chondrites keep their presolar grains; any heating that would fractionate the bulk composition would also destroy or isotopically reset the fragile grains. **(d)** Presolar grains are identified only by **atom-scale isotopic analysis of the physical grain** in a mass spectrometer — you must dissolve the rock, isolate the micron residue, and measure single grains. Remote sensing measures light from bulk surfaces and averages over everything; it can never isolate one micron-sized grain, still less measure its per-atom isotope ratios. This capability exists only because we hold the rock.

**9 — Open problem.** **(a)** Candidates: **Shock waves** (nebular gas shocks) reproduce the required peak temperatures and hours-long cooling well and can process large volumes, but struggle to explain the compound chondrules and the fine details of repeated heating of the same grains. **Lightning** naturally gives brief, intense, local heating (matching the flash), but it is unclear whether disk conditions build enough charge, and it struggles to process the enormous total mass of chondrules. **The X-wind** explains transport of high-temperature material outward and links chondrules to CAIs near the Sun, but has trouble matching the observed cooling rates and the fact that chondrules and their host matrix share a common, un-transported chemistry. **Collisions** between molten/warm planetesimals explain some textures and the crowding, but are hard to reconcile with chondrules that predate large bodies and with the sheer fraction of all inner-disk solids that are chondrules. Each explains the peak temperature; each struggles with either the mass budget, the repetition, or the timing. **(b)** A discriminating dataset: **precise formation ages of many individual chondrules relative to CAIs**, plus their relationship to when large parent bodies existed. A *nebular* mechanism predicts chondrule formation spread across the first ~1–3 Myr, overlapping CAI time and *predating* most large planetesimals; an *impact* mechanism predicts chondrules form *later*, only after bodies grew large enough to collide molten, i.e. after a delay, and correlated with the differentiation ages of parent bodies. High-precision chronology of a large chondrule population — do they cluster early (nebular) or late and post-planetesimal (impact)? — is the cleanest discriminator, complemented by whether chondrule compositions match a well-mixed nebula or the specific bodies impacts would splash. **(c)** Fine **dust and micrometeorites** dominate the delivered *mass* (see the estimate below) and, being decelerated gently high in the atmosphere, arrive *unheated* — so their organics survive, whereas a large impactor is largely vaporised on impact. That argues the *steady, surviving* organic delivery to the early Earth was dominated by fine dust, integrated over billions of years and the whole planet's surface. But large impactors deliver rare, concentrated pulses and can locally emplace much material at once. What would settle it: measuring the intact organic content of interplanetary dust particles and micrometeorites versus the fraction of impactor organics that survives large-body entry and impact — a mass-times-survival-fraction budget for each channel. **(d)** Both are **population-and-history questions, not single-object questions.** Chondrule origin needs the ages and properties of *many* chondrules across *many* meteorites plus disk models — no single spacecraft visits the early disk, and no pure theory can pick among mechanisms that all reach 1,800 K without the data to constrain timing. Organic delivery needs the integrated flux and survival statistics of an entire population of dust and impactors over 4.5 Gyr — again a distribution, measured across many samples, not a fact any one mission or calculation can supply. As with the dilute core of Jupiter, these are questions about populations and histories, and they need populations and histories to answer.

*(Mass-flux estimate for 9c: Earth accretes ~$4\times10^{7}$ kg yr$^{-1}$ of extraterrestrial material, dominated by ~100–200 μm micrometeorites. Recoverable meteorite falls contribute of order $10^{4}$ kg yr$^{-1}$ at most — a ratio of ~$10^{3}$ to $10^{4}$ in favour of dust. The quiet rain outweighs the fireballs by thousands to one.)*$astroMet_master$,
    true,
    true
  )
  on conflict (node_id, depth, level, format) do update
    set body = excluded.body, generated = excluded.generated, reviewed = excluded.reviewed
    where content.body is distinct from excluded.body
       or content.generated is distinct from excluded.generated
       or content.reviewed is distinct from excluded.reviewed
  returning node_id
