where node_id in (select node_id from master)
  and not (depth = 'research' and level = 'advanced' and format = 'read');

with q as (
  select $astroMet_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The chapter says the single split that organises all of meteorite classification is 'did the parent body melt or not.' Which statement correctly maps a meteorite type onto that split?", "options": ["An iron meteorite comes from a body that never melted, since pure metal cannot form by igneous processes and must be a primitive condensate.", "A carbonaceous chondrite is an achondrite whose chondrules were destroyed by extensive melting and recrystallisation.", "A chondrite is a rock that was never melted as a whole - a compacted sediment of nebular particles - whereas achondrites, irons, and stony-irons all come from parent bodies that melted and differentiated into crust, core, and boundary layers.", "All three chondrite clans (carbonaceous, ordinary, enstatite) come from differentiated bodies, distinguished only by how deep in the crust they formed."], "answerIndex": 2, "explanation": "The backbone of the classification is whether the parent body ever got hot enough to melt and separate metal from rock. A chondrite never melted as a whole: it is a physical accumulation - a sediment - of silicate dust, metal grains, sulphides, and millimetre chondrules pressed together, still carrying its ingredients side by side as the disk delivered them. Everything else comes from a body that DID melt and differentiate: achondrites are fragments of the silicate crust and mantle (igneous rock, chondrules destroyed), iron meteorites are pieces of the sunk metallic core, and stony-irons (pallasites, mesosiderites) sample the transition zones such as the core-mantle boundary. Option A inverts this - irons are the melted core, the most thoroughly processed material, not a primitive condensate. Option B confuses the two great families. Option D is wrong because all three chondrite clans are unmelted; they differ by where and under what conditions they assembled in the disk, not by crustal depth."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why do CAIs in the Allende meteorite, dated to 4.567 billion years, serve as 'time zero' for the solar system, and how is that age measured to a hundredth of a percent?", "options": ["CAIs are the oldest solids because they are made of the most refractory minerals - the first to condense from a cooling gas of solar composition above about 1,400 K - so their formation marks the first appearance of solid matter in the disk; the age comes from lead-lead (Pb-Pb) dating, which divides the two uranium-decay equations so the radiogenic 207Pb/206Pb ratio depends only on age.", "CAIs are the oldest solids because they contain the most uranium of any meteoritic mineral, and the age is read directly from the total amount of lead they have accumulated.", "CAIs mark time zero because they are the youngest inclusions in Allende, formed after the chondrules, and are dated by counting their fusion-crust layers.", "CAIs are dated by matching their reflectance spectrum to the Sun's photosphere, which fixes the age at 4.567 Gyr by the cosmochemical abundance standard."], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "CAIs (calcium-aluminium-rich inclusions) are made of corundum, melilite, spinel, and perovskite - the most refractory oxides, which condense first from a cooling gas of solar composition, above roughly 1,400 K, before anything you could stand on. They are therefore the first solids the disk made, which is why they are the OLDEST rather than merely old, and every other age in the course (lunar basalts, iron parent bodies, Earth) is quoted relative to their condensation. The precision comes from Pb-Pb dating: 238U decays to 206Pb (half-life 4.47 Gyr) and 235U to 207Pb (half-life 0.704 Gyr). Dividing one decay equation by the other cancels the uranium abundances, and since present-day 238U/235U is a fixed constant (about 137.8), the radiogenic 207Pb/206Pb ratio depends only on age t. Measuring a ratio of two lead isotopes is far more precise than measuring absolute quantities, giving 4,567.3 +/- 0.2 Myr. Option B is wrong because you never need the initial or absolute U/Pb amounts. Option C inverts the age ordering and invents a dating method. Option D confuses the CI-Sun compositional match with radiometric dating."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The chapter presents chondrules as one of its central unsolved problems. What exactly is unexplained about them?", "options": ["Why chondrules are found only in differentiated achondrites and never in primitive chondrites.", "Chondrules are millimetre-scale silicate droplets that were once completely molten and froze into spheres, and their textures require repeated flash-heating to about 1,800 K followed by cooling over hours - yet a millimetre droplet radiating freely into space would cool in seconds, and no established mechanism can supply that repeated heating to a large fraction of all inner-disk solids.", "Why chondrules contain isotopic ratios hundreds of times outside the solar system's range, marking them as older than the Sun.", "Why chondrules failed to melt despite temperatures reaching 1,800 K in the solar nebula."], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Chondrules make up more than half the mass of a typical ordinary chondrite. Each is a bead of silicate that was once completely molten and cooled into a free-floating sphere - the shapes and internal crystal textures are unambiguous. To make one you must flash-heat nebular dust to roughly 1,800 K (enough to melt silicate), hold it briefly, then let it cool over hours (tens to thousands of K/hr), and do this again and again for a large fraction of all inner-disk solids over a few million years. There is a second constraint: a millimetre droplet radiating freely into cold space would cool in seconds, not hours, so chondrules must have formed in dense crowds where surrounding hot solids and gas held the cooling down to the leisurely rate the textures demand. The honest admission is that after more than a century of study, the heating mechanism remains unidentified - shock waves, lightning, the X-wind, and planetesimal collisions are all plausible and none established. Option A inverts where chondrules occur (they define chondrites). Option C describes presolar grains, a different result. Option D is backwards - the puzzle is that they DID melt, repeatedly."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "How were the SNC (shergottite-nakhlite-chassignite) meteorites shown to come from Mars, and what makes that identification convincing?", "options": ["Their reflectance spectra were matched to Mars by the Dawn spacecraft, which orbited the planet and found the impact basin they were blasted from.", "They were traced to Mars because their crystallisation ages exceed 4 billion years, matching the age of the Martian crust.", "They contain presolar silicon carbide grains whose isotopic ratios match dust measured in the Martian atmosphere by the Viking landers.", "Some shergottites contain impact glass that trapped a pocket of the atmosphere the rock sat in when it was launched; the noble-gas and nitrogen isotope ratios in that trapped gas - argon, heavy nitrogen, xenon, krypton - match the Martian atmosphere measured directly by the Viking landers in 1976, point for point, and their young crystallisation ages (under 200 Myr) already rule out any small asteroid."], "answerIndex": 3, "explanation": "The SNCs are young igneous rocks, some crystallised less than 200 million years ago - far too recent for any small asteroid, which froze solid in the first tens of millions of years once its short-lived 26Al heat was gone. Only a body still geologically active recently, a planet, could produce them. The decisive proof came from gas: some shergottites contain impact-melted glass with trapped pockets of the atmosphere the rock was sitting in when struck. The argon-isotope ratios, the enrichment in heavy nitrogen, and the xenon and krypton abundances match the Martian atmosphere measured by the Viking landers in 1976, point for point. A match across several independent isotope ratios at once is effectively a fingerprint - no other body shares that whole pattern - which is far stronger than any single ratio matching. Option A describes the HED-Vesta link (Dawn orbited Vesta, not Mars). Option B inverts the ages - youth, not antiquity, is the clue. Option C confuses the SNC gas argument with presolar grains."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "The Bennu sample returned by OSIRIS-REx contained water-soluble evaporite salts (sodium carbonates, sulphates, phosphates, chlorides). Why is it argued that no meteorite from Bennu could ever have preserved these salts, however promptly it was recovered?", "options": ["Because evaporite salts are wildly water-soluble, so the first terrestrial rain or even humid air after a fall would leach them out - the damage is done by Earth's water before any collector reaches the stone - whereas a sample kept dry from asteroid to laboratory can still hold them as direct chemical fossils of ancient brines.", "Because evaporite salts are destroyed by the shock of the launching impact and so never leave the parent body inside a meteorite.", "Because meteorites from Bennu would be too small to contain a measurable quantity of salt.", "Because the salts sublimate away during atmospheric entry heating, which a sealed capsule avoids by keeping the sample below its melting point."], "answerIndex": 0, "explanation": "Evaporite salts are the minerals that crystallise when a brine evaporates, and they are extremely water-soluble. A meteorite carrying them would have them washed clean out by the first rain - or even humid air - it met on Earth, so the loss happens through terrestrial weathering regardless of how quickly the stone is found; promptness cannot help because Earth's water reaches the salts before the collector does. Only a sample kept dry from the surface of the asteroid all the way to a nitrogen glovebox, never passing through the atmosphere, can still hold them. They are direct chemical fossils of ancient brines - pools of salty water on a body a few hundred metres across, four and a half billion years ago. This is exactly why sample return beats a fall: it closes the four wounds (shock heating, ablation, weathering, and biological contamination) that a meteorite always arrives with. Option B misidentifies the killing process as launch shock rather than terrestrial water. Option D invents an entry-heating loss; the point is the water contact on the ground, and a returned sample avoids the atmosphere entirely rather than merely staying cool."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "The chapter calls a meteorite 'a sample with no address.' Explain what that phrase means, why it is a fundamental limitation rather than a minor inconvenience, and how sample return (Genesis, Stardust, Hayabusa, Hayabusa2/Ryugu, OSIRIS-REx/Bennu) is designed to overcome it. In your answer, connect the limitation specifically to the claims about organics and prebiotic chemistry.", "rubric": "A strong answer explains that 'a sample with no address' means a meteorite arrives anonymous and degraded: for the overwhelming majority of meteorites - including nearly every chondrite that carries the CAIs, chondrules, and presolar grains - we do not know which parent body they came from. It should list the four ways a meteorite is degraded: (1) shock-heated by the launching collision and again on atmospheric entry, scrambling the fine thermal record; (2) stripped of soluble and volatile components by atmospheric ablation; (3) weathered by terrestrial water and oxygen, so soluble minerals dissolve and metal rusts; and (4) contaminated by terrestrial biology and organic molecules within hours of landing. The answer should note that for the coarse story (melted or not, iron or stone, roughly how old) this is enough, but for the finest question - what fragile organic and aqueous chemistry the early solar system actually made - it is fatal, because you could never prove an amino acid in a museum meteorite came from space rather than from the hands that collected it. Sample return closes all four wounds at once: fly to a NAMED body, collect a DOCUMENTED amount, seal it against contamination, and bring it home WITHOUT passing through an atmosphere (the entry heat is taken by the capsule's shield while the sample stays cold), with witness plates measuring any leaked terrestrial trace. A strong answer names the campaign - Genesis (solar wind, capsule crashed but recovered the Sun's O and N isotopes), Stardust (comet 81P/Wild 2 dust in aerogel, found high-temperature inner-disk grains showing large-scale mixing), Hayabusa (Itokawa, first asteroid sample, linked ordinary chondrites to common stony asteroids), Hayabusa2 (about 5 g of carbonaceous Ryugu including subsurface material), and OSIRIS-REx (about 120 g of carbonaceous Bennu). It should close by tying this to organics: Ryugu and Bennu, curated in nitrogen and never exposed to air, yielded amino acids (common and rare), all five DNA/RNA nucleobases in Bennu, aqueous-alteration minerals, and water-soluble evaporite salts - precisely the contamination-prone, soluble chemistry a fall destroys - so the fifty-year contamination objection to meteorite organics finally loses its force. The deepest advantage is known provenance and curation, not merely avoiding physical damage."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Presolar grains (silicon carbide, graphite, nanodiamond) are described as 'grains older than the Sun.' Explain the observation that forces this conclusion, why no in-system process can account for it, where the grains actually formed, and why their survival depends on the same property of the host rock that lets CI chondrites match the Sun's composition. Then state why this is a capability unique to holding the rock.", "rubric": "A strong answer explains the observation: when a primitive chondrite is dissolved in acid, a tiny acid-resistant residue of microscopic grains of silicon carbide, graphite, and nanodiamond (tens of nanometres to a few microns) remains, and atom-by-atom mass-spectrometer measurement of their carbon, silicon, nitrogen, and trapped noble-gas isotopes gives ratios off by factors of hundreds or thousands from anything the solar system contains. The answer should argue that every process operating in the solar system - nebular condensation, chondrule melting, planetary geology - started from one well-mixed reservoir with a single isotopic composition and can shift it only slightly, so a ratio off by a factor of several hundred cannot be produced by ANY in-system process; it must be inherited from outside. It should state where they formed: in the cooling outflows of stars that died before the Sun was born - AGB red giants (heavy-element patterns from slow neutron capture, dredged-up carbon blown off in winds) and supernovae (products of explosive nucleosynthesis) - condensing as stardust that drifted through the interstellar medium into the collapsing cloud that became the solar system. Each isotopic signature is a fingerprint of its stellar factory. Crucially, the grains survive only because their host chondrite never got hot enough to destroy or isotopically reset them: the SAME coolness (never melted, minimally metamorphosed or aqueously altered) that lets CI chondrites keep the Sun's element abundances element-for-element lets the most primitive chondrites keep their presolar grains - any heating that would fractionate the bulk composition would also erase the grains. Finally, a strong answer explains that this is unique to holding the rock: presolar grains are identified only by isolating a micron-sized physical grain and measuring its per-atom isotope ratios in a mass spectrometer. Remote sensing measures bulk-surface light and averages over everything; it can never isolate one micron grain or measure single-atom isotope ratios. These are physical samples of other stars, delivered to a laboratory bench and older than the ground we stand on, obtainable only by dissolving the rock and reading what is left."}]$astroMet_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/solar-system/small-bodies/meteorites', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
